  "body": "Found by probing the values rather than reading the code. Two things were wrong with the settings promoted in 4.50.0.\n\n## A blank value refused to start the server\n\n```\ncase              outcome    result\nabsent            loads      120.0\nblank             REFUSES    \"Input should be a valid number\"\n```\n\nThe admin UI writes `KEY=` for a cleared field, and a hand-edited file can hold the same — so \"stop specifying this\" was indistinguishable from \"this is broken\". Blank now means the default, which is what clearing a field is asking for.\n\n## Nothing was bounded\n\n```\ncompression 42     loads     REQUEST_LOG_COMPRESSION_LEVEL = 42\nretries -4         loads     PROVIDER_RETRY_ATTEMPTS = -4\nholdback -1        loads     STREAM_COMMIT_HOLDBACK_SECONDS = -1.0\ndeadline 99999999  loads     FALLBACK_FIRST_TOKEN_TIMEOUT = 99999999.0\n```\n\nLevel 42 validated as **valid** and then failed on every body write — zstd's real ceiling is 22, so request logging would have broken silently.\n\n## One table, three readers\n\n`config/limits.py` holds the ranges. Settings clamps to it so the server always starts, the manifest publishes it so the number input carries `min`/`max` and the help says *\"Accepts 1 to 22.\"*, and the admin validator quotes it back rather than silently clamping what someone typed.\n\nThe two answers to a bad value are deliberately different: a **form** can reject it while the user is looking at it; a **file** edited by hand is only discovered at boot, where refusing to start is worse than running a sane value and logging the change.\n\nBounds are wide on purpose — they rule out values that cannot work, not values someone might reasonably want. A provider always gets at least one attempt; a holdback above 30s is latency rather than a recovery window; `0` keeps its documented meaning wherever it had one.\n\n## Tests\n\n88 new, parametrized over every limit: blank falls back, absent falls back, below-range clamps, above-range clamps, each default sits inside its own range, the form publishes the same bounds the server clamps to, and zstd genuinely rejects `max + 1`.\n\nFailure sets by test ID against untouched `main`: **60 before, 60 after, zero new.**\n\n🤖 Generated with [Claude Code](https://claude.com/claude-code)",
