    },
    "message": "feat(config): give every limit a usable range and a working fallback\n\nTwo things were wrong with the settings promoted in 4.50.0, both found by\nprobing the values rather than reading the code.\n\nA blank value refused to start the server. The admin UI writes `KEY=` for a\ncleared field and a hand-edited file can hold the same, so \"stop specifying\nthis\" was indistinguishable from \"this is broken\". Blank now means the default,\nwhich is what someone clearing a field is asking for.\n\nNothing was bounded. REQUEST_LOG_COMPRESSION_LEVEL=42 validated as fine and\nthen failed on every body write, because zstd's real ceiling is 22; negative\nretry counts, a negative holdback and a 99999999s deadline all loaded happily.\n\nRanges now live in one table read by three places that have to agree: Settings\nclamps to it so the server always starts, the manifest publishes it so the\nnumber input carries min/max and the help text says what it accepts, and the\nadmin validator quotes it back instead of silently clamping what someone typed.\n\nThe two ways of answering a bad value are deliberately different. A form can\nreject it while the user is looking at it. A file edited by hand is only\ndiscovered at boot, where refusing to start is worse than running a sane value\nand logging that it was changed.\n\nBounds are wide on purpose: they rule out values that cannot work, not values\nsomeone might reasonably want.\n\nMINOR: new validation behaviour and published field bounds, backward compatible.",
